                    text: "**Transmission first.** The National Electricity Plan for 2022–32 budgets about ₹9.2 lakh crore for transmission and takes 220 kV-and-above transformation from about 1,451 GVA in FY26 to about 2,412 GVA by FY32, roughly 9% a year, with 765 kV substation capacity about tripling. A newer CEA plan (March 2026) adds 8,27,600 MVA of substations and ₹7.93 lakh crore to 2035-36 to connect 664 GW of solar and wind. ICRA expects ₹5–6 trillion of transmission capex over FY27–FY32, which it says needs about 120 GVA of substations a year. The urgency is real: by May 2026 about a third of the 54.8 GW of recently built renewable capacity was running on temporary grid access, with 50–60% of output curtailed in solar hours. **Distribution** adds steady volume through the ₹3 lakh crore RDSS scheme, and **data centres** add a new buyer: CBRE counts about 1.7 GW in India in 2025 and Wood Mackenzie expects 12 GW by 2030. **My view: evacuation, not generation, is now the bottleneck, and that is directly bullish for transformers.**",
